.TH WRAITH 1 "" "Wraith" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wraith \- inspect syscall provenance for selected Linux processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wraith doctor
.RB [ --json ]
.br
.B wraith run
.RI [ options ] " -- program " [ arguments ...]
.br
.B wraith attach
.RI [ options ] " pid"
.br
.B wraith scan
.RI [ options ]
.RB ( --match
.I substring
.RB | --all )
.SH DESCRIPTION
Wraith is a native Linux x86-64 runtime sensor implemented in Rust. It uses
ptrace syscall stops and procfs mappings to inspect executable origins,
protection requests and optional stack placement, and correlates those signals
within a process address space. Observe-only is the default.
.PP
A clean verdict means no configured signal was observed, not that a process
is uncompromised. JIT engines, alternate stacks and other legitimate runtimes
can resemble injection. Review representative benign behavior before enforcement.
File-backed code reuse and data-only attacks can evade origin-based rules.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B doctor
Check native platform, procfs and actual ptrace operation on an owned child;
report Yama policy. Use --json for a machine-readable readiness report.
.TP
.B run
Launch a foreground program, following its threads and descendants.
The program and its arguments follow --.
.TP
.B attach
Attach to a running process and its existing thread group, subject to the
permissions already enforced by Linux.
.TP
.B scan
Attach to matching processes. Select --match or --all explicitly; prefer a
small --max-targets value rather than tracing an entire busy host.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json FILE
Append JSONL detections to FILE. A dash writes JSON to stdout; in run mode,
target stdout is routed to stderr to keep the evidence stream machine-readable.
For doctor, --json takes no file argument and prints its readiness report.
.TP
.B --min SEVERITY
Report info, warn, high or critical events at or above the selected floor.
This filters output, not the detector, enforcement or final verdict.
.TP
.B --correlation-window NUMBER
Set the syscall-event correlation window from 1 to 1000000; the default is 64.
Correlation also has a 30-second wall-clock time-to-live. This bound controls
signal aging, not a guarantee that every exploitation chain is detected.
.TP
.B --max-history NUMBER
Retain at most 0 to 10000 exited-process histories; the default is 128.
Active process state is always retained. A value of zero disables retired history,
not monitoring or current detections.
.TP
.B --jit-critical
Select an explicit no-JIT policy. Default anonymous RX execution and W-to-X
transitions are warnings; this policy can elevate them. Do not enable blindly
for legitimate runtime-generated code.
.TP
.B --trust-region START-END
Exempt an operator-vouched half-open hexadecimal address interval. A trust
range is an intentional coverage gap, not attestation. Protection exemptions
must cover the effective ordinary page-rounded span. Huge-page arenas require
additional care and should not be trusted through these exemptions.
.TP
.B --no-stack-pivot
Disable the custom-stack-sensitive placement heuristic.
.TP
.B --audit-sensitive
Log sensitive syscalls from accepted executable origins as informational events.
.TP
.B --block
On a critical event, skip the offending syscall so it returns ENOSYS.
.TP
.B --kill
On a critical event, terminate the traced process tree. Mutually exclusive
with --block. Failed enforcement may trigger fail-closed termination.
.TP
.B --match SUBSTRING
In scan mode, select process names or command lines containing SUBSTRING.
.TP
.B --max-targets NUMBER
Limit the number of selected scan targets.
.TP
.B --all
In scan mode, explicitly select all processes the tracer is allowed to attach to.
.TP
.B --ui
Use the live terminal dashboard.
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress human detection lines. Diagnostic errors and final outcomes remain visible.
.TP
.BR --version , " -V"
Print the package's upstream version.
.TP
.BR --help , " -h"
Print command help.
.SH EXIT STATUS
For monitoring commands, 0 means no high/critical event, 1 means high activity,
2 means usage/tracing/evidence-output failure, and 3 means critical activity.
The root target's exit or signal is reported separately; it does not replace
the sensor exit policy. Mapping coverage failures are operational failures and
yield status 2 even when coverage subsequently recovers. Output severity filtering
does not erase an observed coverage failure or convert it into a clean verdict.
.PP
Doctor returns 0 when owned-child tracing and required platform/procfs checks
are usable, or 2 for failed readiness or usage. An unhealthy JSON report does
not by itself mean package installation failed.
.SH REQUIREMENTS AND LIMITS
Linux kernel 5.3 or newer on native amd64, mounted procfs and appropriate
ptrace permission are required. 32-bit and x32 tracees are unsupported.
Every syscall pays stop/resume overhead. This is focused monitoring, not
low-overhead whole-host telemetry or a complete endpoint product.
.PP
Launched targets use EXITKILL; unexpected tracer death kills its owned tree.
Unsupported launched job-control group stops return an operational error rather
than being silently bypassed. Use service/foreground workflows, not transparent
interactive job control. Attached targets do not receive automatic capabilities
or changes to system security policy from this package.
